[[File:Telopea aspera (habit).jpg|thumb|225px|''T. aspera'' (habit)]]

'''''Telopea aspera''''', commonly known as '''Gibraltar Range waratah''',<ref name="NSW" /> is a plant in the family [[Proteaceae]]. It grows as a woody shrub to {{convert|3|m|0}} high with leathery rough leaves and bright red flower heads known as [[inflorescence]]s—each composed of hundreds of individual flowers. It is endemic to the [[New England (New South Wales)|New England]] region in [[New South Wales]] in [[Australia]]. It was formally described as a species by botanists Peter Weston and Mike Crisp in 1995, separated from its close relative ''[[Telopea speciosissima]]'' by its rough foliage and preference for drier habitat. Unlike its better known relative, ''Telopea aspera'' has rarely been cultivated.

==Description==
''Telopea aspera'' is a large erect shrub up to {{convert|3|m|0}} tall with one or more stems. It has dull green leaves which are alternate, and are more coarsely-toothed than its southern relative, with 3 to 11 serrations on each leaf margin. Measuring {{convert|8|–|28|cm}} long and {{convert|2|–|6.5|cm|1}} wide, the leaves are tough and leathery with furry undersurfaces. There are prominent veins on both the upper and lower leaf surfaces. The [[inflorescence]]s, which appear in spring, are large and crimson coloured. They consist of a large domed flowerhead ringed by bracts. There are anywhere from 90 to 250 individual flowers making up the flower head. These are followed by [[Follicle (fruit)|follicles]] {{cvt|70–110|mm}} long.<ref name=NSW>{{cite web|last1=Crisp |first1=Michael D. |last2=Weston |first2=Peter H. |title=''Telopea aspera'' (Sm.) R.Br.|work=New South Wales Flora Online|url=http://plantnet.rbgsyd.nsw.gov.au/cgi-bin/NSWfl.pl?page=nswfl&lvl=sp&name=Telopea~aspera|access-date=8 January 2015}}</ref><ref name="ABRS">{{cite web |title=''Telopea aspera'' |url=https://profiles.ala.org.au/opus/foa/profile/Telopea%20aspera |publisher=Australian Biological Resources Study, Department of Agriculture, Water and the Environment: Canberra |access-date=21 January 2022}}</ref>

==Taxonomy==
''Telopea aspera'' is one of five species from south eastern Australia which make up the genus [[Waratah|''Telopea'']]. Its closest relative is the very similar New South Wales waratah (''T.&nbsp;speciosissima'') from the Sydney region in central New South Wales, from which it was only recognised as a separate species in 1991, having previously been considered an unusual northern population.<ref name=FoA/> Initially provisionally called ''Telopea'' sp. A,<ref name=APNI1>{{cite web|title=''Telopea'' sp. 'A'|url= https://id.biodiversity.org.au/instance/apni/665458 |publisher=APNI|access-date=21 January 2022}}</ref> it was formally described as ''Telopea aspera'' in 1995 by [[Michael Crisp]] and [[Peter Henry Weston|Peter Weston]] in the journal ''[[Telopea (journal)|Telopea]]''.<ref name=APNI>{{cite web|title=''Telopea aspera''|url= https://id.biodiversity.org.au/instance/apni/558344 |publisher=APNI|access-date=21 January 2022}}</ref><ref name=FoA>{{cite book|last1=Crisp |first1=Michael D. |last2=Weston |first2=Peter H. |year=1995|chapter=''Telopea''|editor-last=McCarthy |editor-first=Patrick |title=Flora of Australia: Volume 16: Eleagnaceae, Proteaceae 1|pages=386–390|publisher=CSIRO Publishing / Australian Biological Resources Study|isbn=0-643-05693-9}}</ref> The species name is derived from the [[Latin]] adjective {{Lang|la|asper}} "rough", and relates to the leaves.<ref name="ANPSA">{{cite web|url=http://anpsa.org.au/t-asp.html|title=''Telopea aspera''|last=Walters|first=Brian|date=October 2008|work=Plant Guides|publisher=Australian Native Plants Society (Australia)|access-date=8 January 2015|archive-date=26 July 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150726171012/http://anpsa.org.au/t-asp.html|url-status=dead}}</ref>

The genus is classified in the subtribe [[Grevilleoideae|Embothriinae]] of the Proteaceae, along with the [[Alloxylon|tree waratahs (''Alloxylon'')]] from eastern Australia and New Caledonia, and ''[[Oreocallis]]'' and the [[Embothrium coccineum|Chilean firetree (''Embothrium coccineum'')]] from South America.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Johnson |first1=L. A. S. |last2=Briggs |first2=Barbara G. | year = 1975 | title = On the Proteaceae: the evolution and classification of a southern family | journal = Botanical Journal of the Linnean Society | volume = 70 | issue = 2 | pages = 83–182 | doi = 10.1111/j.1095-8339.1975.tb01644.x}}</ref><ref name="Weston 2006">{{cite journal |last1=Weston |first1=Peter H. |last2=Barker |first2=Nigel P. | year = 2006 | title = A new suprageneric classification of the Proteaceae, with an annotated checklist of genera | journal = [[Telopea (journal)|Telopea]] | volume = 11 | issue = 3 | pages = 314–344|doi=10.7751/telopea20065733 }}</ref> Almost all these species have red terminal flowers, and hence the subtribe's origin and floral appearance must predate the splitting of [[Gondwana]] into Australia, Antarctica, and South America over 60''&nbsp;''million years ago.<ref name="Nixon" />{{rp|19}}

==Distribution and habitat==
Gibraltar Range waratah is endemic to northern New South Wales, where it is restricted to the [[Gibraltar Range]]. Found in dry sclerophyll forest,<ref name=NSW/><!-- cites both previous sentences --> it is a component of three plant communities within Gibraltar Range: the first is composed of Gibraltar ash (''[[Eucalyptus olida]]''), privet-leaved stringybark (''[[Eucalyptus ligustrina]]'') and diehard stringybark (''[[Eucalyptus cameronii]]'') and occurs on slopes and crests, while the second is composed of Gibraltar ash, large-fruited blackbutt (''[[Eucalyptus pyrocarpa]]'') and needlebark stringybark (''[[Eucalyptus planchoniana]]'') and occurs on ridges and north and west-facing slopes. Both are shrubby to open woodland communities found on skeletal to shallow soil on [[granite]]. The third community is a more sheltered one of New England blackbutt (''[[Eucalyptus campanulata]]'') and diehard stringybark  found on lower slopes on sandy or loam soils.<ref name="Hunter">{{cite journal|last1=Hunter|first1=John T.|last2=Sheringham |first2=Paul |date=2008|title=Vegetation and floristic diversity in Gibraltar Range and part of Washpool National Parks, New South Wales|journal=Cunninghamia|volume=10|issue=3|pages=439–74|url=http://core.kmi.open.ac.uk/download/pdf/14529069.pdf}}</ref>

==Ecology==

The species was added to the Australian national list of threatened species on 4 September 2024.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Linder |first=Esther |date=4 September 2024 |title=Waratah species added to threatened list alongside geckos and crayfish as Labor's environment overhaul stalls |url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-09-04/20-species-on-threatened-list-gibraltar-range-waratah/104307482 |access-date=4 September 2024 |work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]] |language=en}}</ref>

This waratah resprouts from a woody lignotuber after bushfire.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Croft |first1=Peter |last2=Hofmeyer |first2=Damien |last3=Hunter |first3=John T. |date=2006 |title=Fire Responses in Four Rare Plant Species at Gibraltar Range |journal=Proc. Linn. Soc. N.S.W. |volume=127 |pages=57–62 |url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/279916391}}</ref> It stores energy and nutrients as a resource for rapid growth after a bushfire.<ref name="Nixon">{{Cite book |last=Nixon |first=Paul |title=The Waratah |date=1989 |publisher=Kangaroo Press |isbn=0-86417-878-6 |edition=2nd |location=East Roseville, New South Wales}}</ref>{{rp|25–26}}

The prominent position and striking colour of most members of the subtribe [[Embothriinae]] both in Australia and South America strongly suggest they are adapted to pollination by birds, and have been for over 60 million years.<ref name="Nixon" />{{rp|19}}

==Use in horticulture==
''Telopea aspera'' has been rarely cultivated, though it is possible that some garden specimens thought to be ''T.&nbsp;speciosissima'' may in fact have been this species. Plants have fared poorly at [[Australian Botanic Garden Mount Annan]], perishing in summer, though the reason for this is not known. Cultivation of ''T.&nbsp;aspera'' at Mount Tomah Botanic Gardens has been more successful. The flowers and foliage are attractive horticultural features.<ref name="ANPSA"/><!-- cites 4 previous sentences -->

